A model's quantities say what is uncertain; its **terms** say how. A term is a log-density, built
from the model's quantities by [operations](../operations/) like any other value, and the model's
density is the sum of its terms. This page describes terms, the probabilistic graph they make with
the quantities, and what a snapshot can say about it.

## A term

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/term.rs:16..27}
```

A term is added in one of two ways:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/term.rs:274..296}
```

`score` adds the term of the distribution a quantity was drawn from: its density, as a function of
the quantity it **scores** and the quantities it is **conditioned on**. `factor` adds a
**likelihood factor**, which scores nothing: the likelihood of data that is not a quantity of the
model. Either way, the log-density must be computed by an operation and be a `Real`.

Which quantities a term involves is never declared. It is read off the
[compilation graph](../building/#the-compilation-graph): the quantities among the roots its
log-density is computed from. Two rules keep the model generative. A quantity is scored by at most
one term, the distribution it was drawn from. And a term is conditioned only on quantities made
before the one it scores, so the order in which quantities were made is an ancestral order, in
which each comes after everything its distribution depends on. A quantity no term scores is a
**parameter**, such as a sensor reading the model is driven by.

## An example

A vehicle at $x_0$ and then $x_1$ measures a landmark $m$ once from each position, as $z_0$ and
$z_1$. Its motion is driven by an inertial reading $\zeta$, and a second motion model is a
likelihood factor over $x_0$, $x_1$ and a scale $\sigma$:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/tests/queries.rs:31..57}
```

Five terms score the five quantities $x_0$, $m$, $x_1$, $z_0$ and $z_1$; $\zeta$ and $\sigma$ are
parameters; `t6` and `t7` are likelihood factors, the second an improper prior on $\sigma$.

## The probabilistic graph

The quantities and terms make the model's **probabilistic graph**. Its nodes are the quantities and
the terms; it has an edge from each quantity a term is conditioned on to the term, and from each
term to the quantity it scores. Every edge joins a quantity and a term, and the graph is acyclic,
by the rule above. For the example:

<ModelGraph spec={probabilistic} />

Inference reads this graph rather than being told about it. A snapshot answers the questions an
algorithm asks of it, among them the **Markov blanket** of some quantities, which is what a sampler
or a sliding window needs: the other quantities the same terms involve, so that the quantities'
conditional density, given everything else, is the sum of the terms they are joined to, up to a
constant. For the first tick of the example:

<Walkthrough spec={blanket}>

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro-core/src/model.rs:235..278}
```

</Walkthrough>

The mathematics of all this is in the note on [generative models](../../math/generative-models/).

A term may also carry a **sampler**, which draws the quantity it scores, and **bindings**, which
mark its parts by role (a residual, a whitening factor) for the algorithms that read them. A
parameter may carry a **proposal**, which draws it when the model is generated.

> [!DECISION] Conditioning belongs to the query
> A term scores a quantity whether or not that quantity is later observed; which quantities are
> observed is part of each query. See
> [position 1 of the charter](../../archive/charter/writeup.md#1-a-model-is-the-joint-conditioning-belongs-to-the-query).
